What is a Portfolio for?

A developer portfolio is a collection of work samples presented specifically to demonstrate capability to a target client or employer. Every item in a portfolio should answer: "Here is a problem I solved and how I solved it." It is not a resume. It is evidence.

The Four Sections Every Developer Portfolio Needs

1. Who You Are (one paragraph)
Your name, what you specialize in, and who you help. Not your life story. One clear paragraph that tells a client in ten seconds whether you are relevant to their problem.

2. What You Build (project showcase)
Three to six projects. Each one shows what problem was solved, what tools were used, and what the output looks like. Code links and live demos where possible.

3. What You Offer (services)
A specific list of services, not a vague claim of skills. "I build data pipelines in Python" is a service. "Python developer" is a category. Day 47 covers this in detail.

4. How to Reach You (contact)
One clear call to action. Email, LinkedIn, or a contact form. Not three different options that create decision fatigue.

Writing the Project Description
Each project entry needs four elements: the problem, the solution, the tools, and the result. Keep it under five sentences. Clients do not read long descriptions. They scan for relevance.

Element	                Question it answers	                                     Example
Problem	        What was broken or missing?	     "Manually tracking 28 days of fitness data took 30 minutes per week."

Solution	        What did you build?	         "Automated daily check-in tool that predicts goal achievement and generates coaching."

Tools	         What technology did you use?	            "Python, scikit-learn, FastAPI, pandas."

Result	              What changed?	             "Reduced reporting time to under 30 seconds. Outputs a full week summary in one command."

Generating a Portfolio Summary with Python

The terminal below generates a formatted portfolio summary from a structured data dictionary. This is the same pattern you would use to auto-generate a portfolio page from a database or CMS.

projects = [
    {
        "title":   "SMP Performance Coach",
        "problem": "Manually tracking fitness data and writing coaching notes took 30+ minutes per day.",
        "solution":"Automated pipeline that trains a classifier on 28 days of data, predicts daily goal achievement, and generates a coaching message.",
        "tools":   ["Python", "scikit-learn", "pandas", "FastAPI"],
        "result":  "Prediction + coaching in under 2 seconds. Exports weekly JSON report.",
        "github":  "github.com/brianotieno/smp-tracker",
        "type":    "ML + API"
    },
    {
        "title":   "Multi-Endpoint API Dashboard",
        "problem": "Three separate data sources with no unified view. Reporting was manual and inconsistent.",
        "solution":"Dashboard that fetches from three endpoints, processes each independently, and outputs a combined summary with protocol comparison.",
        "tools":   ["Python", "requests", "pandas"],
        "result":  "Single command produces a full multi-source report. Reduced prep time by 90%.",
        "github":  "github.com/brianotieno/api-dashboard",
        "type":    "Data Pipeline"
    },
    {
        "title":   "Browser-Based AI Coach",
        "problem": "Clients needed a way to get coaching feedback without installing software.",
        "solution":"Client-side JavaScript tool. Prediction engine and coaching generator run entirely in the browser. No server required.",
        "tools":   ["JavaScript", "DOM API", "Fetch API"],
        "result":  "Zero setup for end users. Works on any device with a browser.",
        "github":  "github.com/brianotieno/browser-coach",
        "type":    "Web Tool"
    },
]

SEPARATOR = "=" * 64

print(SEPARATOR)
print("  DEVELOPER PORTFOLIO SUMMARY")
print(SEPARATOR)

for i, p in enumerate(projects, 1):
    print(f"\nProject {i}: {p['title']}  [{p['type']}]")
    print(f"  Problem:  {p['problem']}")
    print(f"  Solution: {p['solution']}")
    print(f"  Tools:    {', '.join(p['tools'])}")
    print(f"  Result:   {p['result']}")
    print(f"  Code:     {p['github']}")

print()
print(SEPARATOR)
print(f"  Total projects: {len(projects)}")
all_tools = set(t for p in projects for t in p["tools"])
print(f"  Tech stack:     {', '.join(sorted(all_tools))}")
print(SEPARATOR)


What Not to Include

What to leave out	                                                  Why
Incomplete or broken projects	                  A broken demo signals unreliability faster than any explanation
Tutorial projects with no modification	          Cloned code shows you can follow directions. Not that you can build.
Projects without a README	                      Undocumented code looks abandoned
More than six projects	                          Dilutes the strongest work. Quality over quantity.
Skills lists without proof	                      "I know Python" means nothing. A Python project means everything.


Note: If you only have one or two solid projects right now, that is enough to start. One finished, documented, working project with a clear README is more compelling than ten unfinished ones. Build the portfolio incrementally as you complete more work.

Where to Host Your Portfolio
Platform	                        Best for	                                                  Cost
GitHub Pages	            Static HTML/CSS portfolio, free hosting from a GitHub repo	          Free
Vercel	                    React or Next.js portfolio, auto-deploys from GitHub	          Free tier available
Render	                    Python/FastAPI backend + static front end together	              Free tier available
Notion	                    Quick portfolio page without code, shareable link	              Free
Custom domain	            Professional presence (e.g., brianotieno.dev)	                  ~$10-15/year

Tip: For a GitHub Pages portfolio, create a repo named username.github.io. Push an index.html file to the main branch. GitHub automatically serves it at https://username.github.io. No server, no cost, live in under five minutes.

